    scripture: {
      reference: "Matthew 10:18-20",
      text: "And you shall be brought before governors, and before kings for my sake, for a testimony to them and to the Gentiles. But when they shall deliver you up, take no thought how or what to speak: for it shall be given you in that hour what to speak. For it is not you that speak, but the Spirit of your Father that speaketh in you.",
    },
    excerpt: {
      workId: "newman-idea",
      author: "John Henry Newman",
      citation: "Apologia Pro Vita Sua, ch. 5",
      text: "It is not at all easy (humanly speaking) to wind up an Englishman to a dogmatic level. He is at home in matter of fact, in lucid narrative, in honest report. But to stand and speak the truth as truth, against the temper of those who hear, and to do it without bitterness — that is another business. The grace which carries a man through it must come from above.",
    },
    meditation:
      "It will happen eventually. After years of taking depositions of other people, you will be the one in the chair. The case is yours from the other side: a malpractice complaint against your firm, a deposition in a partnership dispute, a regulatory inquiry into a representation you handled, a divorce proceeding in which your conduct is at issue. Maybe a grand jury subpoena that has nothing to do with you personally but requires you to answer questions about what you saw. Maybe a deposition in a probate case where you drafted the will. Maybe the bar's disciplinary counsel asking you about a complaint a client filed.\n\nThe shock is real and it is professional rather than emotional. You have always been on one side of the table. The questions have always been your questions. Now they are someone else's questions, and they are slower than you expected and harder than you expected and your training, which is supposed to help, has the perverse effect of making it worse. You can see the technique. You can see what is being set up. You know that you are supposed to answer only what is asked, but in the moment, you can feel the words wanting to leave your mouth in self-defense, and the self-defense is exactly what they are fishing for.\n\nMatthew is not, on its face, about your deposition. It is about the apostles being hauled before governors and kings for the gospel's sake. The promise is that the Holy Spirit will provide the words in the hour of testimony. There is something a little embarrassing about reaching for this verse when the testimony in question is a defense against a fee dispute. The scale is wrong. But the principle is not wrong, and the older saints applied it broadly. The Christian, called to give an account, is given grace to give it. The account may be a small one. The grace is the same kind.\n\nNewman, in the passage above, is writing about the difficulty of standing and speaking the truth as truth against the temper of those who hear, and doing it without bitterness. The line names two things at once. The first is the difficulty of being honest under pressure when the listeners want you to be something other than honest. The second is the difficulty of doing it without bitterness when the listeners are partly your enemies. Lawyers who have been deposed in matters where they are themselves the target know both of these difficulties intimately. Honesty is the easier part. Honesty without bitterness is the part most lawyers fail.\n\nYou were deposed once, in your own divorce, in a deposition that lasted six hours. The questions were sometimes legitimate and sometimes designed to humiliate. The opposing counsel was a lawyer you knew socially and had thought of as a colleague. He was doing what his client wanted. You answered carefully and accurately. You answered without bitterness for the first four hours. In the fifth hour the bitterness came up. You said something that was technically responsive and emotionally barbed. The transcript caught it. The case did not turn on it, but you have remembered it ever since. The bitterness was not in the words. It was in the choice to say them when silence would have served better.\n\nNewman would have recognized that hour. The grace to carry a man through it must come from above. He is not being pious. He is being practical. The natural reserves of patience run out. The professional toolkit does not extend to your own deposition the way it extends to other people's. What is left, when the natural reserves run out, is what the saints have always called grace. The lawyer in their own deposition discovers, sometimes for the first time, that they need it.\n\nThere are practical disciplines, and they are worth naming. Engage your own counsel and let them run the matter. Do not try to be your own lawyer; the deposed lawyer who plays his own counsel is a study in self-defeat. Answer the question that was asked, not the question you wish had been asked, not the question you fear is coming. Do not editorialize. Do not give the speech. Do not assume that the opposing counsel is stupid; they are not, and the speech you give to correct their misunderstanding is mostly a gift to them. Sleep before the deposition. Eat. Take the breaks. The discipline is not heroic. It is what you would tell a client.\n\nThe deeper discipline is the disposition Matthew is naming. You are giving testimony, even if the testimony is about a fee dispute or a representation gone wrong. The hour is, in its small way, an hour of witness. The Spirit that promises words to the apostle in the dock is not absent from the lawyer in the deposition room. The lawyer who can pray before walking in, and who can answer with a settled honesty that does not have to claw for advantage, is doing something the gospel recognizes. The lawyer who can come out of the deposition without bitterness has done something harder than most depositions are designed to allow.\n\nThere is also a particular grace available in being on the other side of the table. You learn, viscerally, what your own witnesses have been going through. The fatigue. The slowness of the clock. The way certain questions seem to be aimed at something you cannot quite see. The temptation to fill the silence. The temptation to be liked by the examiner. The temptation to perform competence. You knew these existed. You did not know what they felt like until you sat in the chair. After your own deposition, you will be a better questioner of other people's depositions, because you will know what the room costs.\n\nA hard line: many lawyers, after being deposed, become quietly more humane in their own depositions of others. Some do not. The ones who do not have learned the lesson backwards. They have decided that, since the room is painful, the painful room is acceptable when administered by them. The ones who do have learned the lesson forwards. They have decided that, since the room is painful, the administration of it requires a care that they did not previously give it.\n\nWhen you are the witness, the deepest formation is the recognition that the chair is the same chair you have used for fifteen years. You sit in it now from the other side. The Spirit who promised the apostle a tongue will provide what you need. The grace, in the older language, is sufficient. The bitterness, if you are alert, can be set down. The witness, even when given under deposition rather than under sword, is still witness, and the One who watched the apostles before the governors is watching you.",
    prayer:
      "Lord, when I am the one in the chair, give me words that are honest and free of bitterness. Settle me before I walk in. Remind me, mid-question, that this hour too is testimony. Let no answer leave my mouth that I will be ashamed to read on the transcript. Amen.",
